# Omarchy Child Protect Guardian — execution allowlist for the child (Threat model §4 L2).
# Drop into /etc/fapolicyd/rules.d/05-guardian.rules then: sudo fagenrules --load && sudo systemctl restart fapolicyd
# (guardian-fapolicyd-setup.sh does this for you.)
#
# WHY 05 (not 50): fapolicyd compiles rules.d in FILENAME order, first match wins. Upstream ships
# 42-trusted-elf / 43-known-elf which DENY any untrusted ELF for EVERY uid. On-host test 2026-10-23
# (F2) showed that at priority 50 those fire first and deny the PARENT's own non-pacman binaries
# (~/.local/.../node, claude) and services (/usr/local/bin/ollama) → broken login / failed boot. At
# 05 our rules run first: we restrict ONLY the child and end with "everyone else: allow", so the
# upstream all-uid denies never fire. Guardian gates the child, not the whole system.
#
# PURPOSE: stop the child running a downloaded AppImage / static ELF / portable binary from $HOME,
# /tmp or a USB stick. Only files fapolicyd TRUSTS (seeded from pacman-owned files + guardian-approved
# installs — see README.md) may be executed by the child.
#
# Rule grammar is `decision perm subject : object`. Trust must be asserted on the OBJECT (the file
# being executed), NOT on the subject (the calling process) — a trusted shell launching an untrusted
# binary must still be denied. First match wins, top to bottom.
#
# !!! DANGER: fapolicyd in enforcing mode can lock the machine out of running programs. ALWAYS test in
# permissive mode first (README) and keep a root shell open. Replace 1001 with `id -u <child>`.

# 1) Root is unrestricted (updates, guardiand, recovery).
allow perm=any uid=0 : all

# 1a) Block the loader-as-launcher laundering trick for the child: `ld-linux.so <untrusted-elf>`.
#     fapolicyd's upstream `ld_so` pattern matches exactly this (review H3). Keep it ABOVE the
#     trust allows so a trusted loader can't be used to run an untrusted target.
deny_audit perm=execute pattern=ld_so uid=1001 : all
# NOTE: memfd-based exec (e.g. Python os.memfd_create) is a separate laundering path. Do NOT set
#       vm.memfd_noexec=2 system-wide — on-host test 2026-10-23 (C0) showed it makes the NEXT BOOT
#       fail (dbus-broker: "memfd_create() requires MFD_NOEXEC_SEAL with vm.memfd_noexec=2" →
#       systemd "Failed to set up API bus"), leaving the host unbootable. The object-trust deny below
#       already blocks running an untrusted memfd'd ELF for the child. If you must tighten memfd,
#       use at most =1 and reboot-test before relying on it.

# 2) The child may execute a file ONLY if that file (the object) is trusted. Untrusted object → deny.
#    This also stops the loader-laundering trick: `ld-linux.so <untrusted-elf>` opens the untrusted
#    object for execute, which this rule denies.
deny_audit perm=execute uid=1001 : all trust=0
allow      perm=execute uid=1001 : all trust=1

# 3) Deny the child opening untrusted ELF shared libraries for execution-mapping (defence in depth).
deny_audit perm=open uid=1001 : all ftype=application/x-sharedlib trust=0

# 4) Everyone else: allow for now (tighten per-account later).
allow perm=any all : all
